            <div xml:id="d673">

                <pb facs="0940" n="840" sameAs="frus:frus1934v01/pg840"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.4731/102: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Sidney</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sydney</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-05-10T17:00:00+10:00">May 10, 1934—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 11—5:05 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Before sending note as instructed have conferred with Trade Commissioner and we
                    both feel some uncertainty concerning possibility of demonstrating that
                    “American imports have suffered greatly by the exception made in favor of
                    Canada”, as stated in second sentence of Department’s telegram of May 5, 2 p.m.
                    It appears to us that favor to Canada has not injured us very greatly, but has
                    merely relieved Canada of a disability imposed by basic Customs Act from which
                    we have suffered not only since but prior to the present Canadian agreement.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caldwell</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
